{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Sentiment Analysis Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: a real mini-dataset"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "POSITIVE = ["
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"absolutely loved this movie\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"beautiful cinematography and a great story\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"one of the best films of the year\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"brilliant acting from the lead\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"heartwarming and funny\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "NEGATIVE = ["
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"boring and far too long\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"not worth your time\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"the plot made no sense\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"terrible acting, awful script\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    \"i want my two hours back\","
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Small on purpose. Real work uses tens of thousands of examples (IMDb, SST-2, Yelp polarity). The math is identical."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: multinomial Naive Bayes from scratch"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "import math"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "from collections import Counter"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def train_nb(docs_by_class, vocab, alpha=1.0):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    class_priors = {}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    class_word_probs = {}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    total_docs = sum(len(d) for d in docs_by_class.values())"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for cls, docs in docs_by_class.items():"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        class_priors[cls] = len(docs) / total_docs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        counts = Counter()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        for doc in docs:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            for token in doc:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                counts[token] += 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        total = sum(counts.values()) + alpha * len(vocab)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        class_word_probs[cls] = {"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            w: (counts[w] + alpha) / total for w in vocab"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        }"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return class_priors, class_word_probs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def predict_nb(doc, class_priors, class_word_probs):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    scores = {}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for cls in class_priors:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        s = math.log(class_priors[cls])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        for token in doc:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            if token in class_word_probs[cls]:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                s += math.log(class_word_probs[cls][token])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        scores[cls] = s"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return max(scores, key=scores.get)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Additive smoothing (alpha=1.0) is Laplace smoothing. Without it, a word unseen in a class has probability zero and the log explodes. `alpha=0.01` is common in practice. `alpha=1.0` is the teaching default."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: logistic regression from scratch"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def sigmoid(x):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-np.clip(x, -20, 20)))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def train_lr(X, y, epochs=500, lr=0.05, l2=0.01):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n_features = X.shape[1]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    w = np.zeros(n_features)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    b = 0.0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for _ in range(epochs):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        logits = X @ w + b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        preds = sigmoid(logits)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        err = preds - y"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        grad_w = X.T @ err / len(y) + l2 * w"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        grad_b = err.mean()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        w -= lr * grad_w"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        b -= lr * grad_b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return w, b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def predict_lr(X, w, b):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return (sigmoid(X @ w + b) >= 0.5).astype(int)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "L2 regularization matters here. Text features are sparse; without L2 the model memorizes training examples. Start at `0.01` and tune."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: handling negation (the failure mode)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Consider \"not good\" and \"not bad\". A BoW classifier sees `{not, good}` and `{not, bad}` and learns from whichever showed up more in training. A bigram classifier sees `not_good` and `not_bad` and learns them as distinct features. That is usually enough."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "A cruder fix that works when you do not have bigrams: **negation scoping**. Prefix tokens following a negation word with `NOT_` up to the next punctuation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "NEGATION_WORDS = {\"not\", \"no\", \"never\", \"nor\", \"none\", \"nothing\", \"neither\"}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "NEGATION_TERMINATORS = {\".\", \"!\", \"?\", \",\", \";\"}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ""
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def apply_negation(tokens):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    out = []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    negate = False"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for token in tokens:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        if token in NEGATION_TERMINATORS:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            negate = False"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            out.append(token)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            continue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        if token in NEGATION_WORDS:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            negate = True"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            out.append(token)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            continue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        out.append(f\"NOT_{token}\" if negate else token)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return out"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        ">>> apply_negation([\"not\", \"good\", \"at\", \"all\", \".\", \"but\", \"funny\"])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "['not', 'NOT_good', 'NOT_at', 'NOT_all', '.', 'but', 'funny']"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Now `good` and `NOT_good` are different features. The classifier can weight them opposite. Three lines of preprocessing, measurable accuracy jump on sentiment benchmarks."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 5: evaluation metrics that matter"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Accuracy alone is misleading if classes are imbalanced. Real sentiment corpora are usually 70-80% positive or 70-80% negative; a constant-majority classifier gets 80% accuracy and is worthless. Report every one of the following:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Per-class precision and recall.** One pair per class. Macro-average them to get a single number that respects class balance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Macro-F1 (primary metric for imbalanced data).** Mean of per-class F1 scores, equally weighted. Use this instead of accuracy when classes are imbalanced."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Weighted-F1 (alternative).** Same as macro but weighted by class frequency. Report alongside macro-F1 when the imbalance itself has business meaning."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Confusion matrix.** Raw counts. Always inspect before trusting any scalar metric; it reveals which pair of classes the model confuses."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- **Per-class error samples.** Pull 5 wrong predictions per class. Read them. Nothing replaces reading the actual errors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "For severely imbalanced data (> 95-5 ratio), report **AUROC** and **AUPRC** instead of accuracy. AUPRC is more sensitive to the minority class, which is what you usually care about (spam, fraud, rare sentiment)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "**Common bug to avoid.** Reporting micro-F1 instead of macro-F1 on imbalanced data gives a number that looks high because it is dominated by the majority class. Macro-F1 forces you to see the minority-class performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def evaluate(y_true, y_pred):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    tp = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 1 and p == 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    fp = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 0 and p == 1)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    fn = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 1 and p == 0)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    tn = sum(1 for t, p in zip(y_true, y_pred) if t == 0 and p == 0)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp else 0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    recall = tp / (tp + fn) if tp + fn else 0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    f1 = 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall) if precision + recall else 0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return {\"tp\": tp, \"fp\": fp, \"tn\": tn, \"fn\": fn, \"precision\": precision, \"recall\": recall, \"f1\": f1}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Easy.** Add `apply_negation` as a preprocessing step in the scikit-learn pipeline and measure the F1 delta on a small sentiment dataset.\n2. **Medium.** Implement class-weighted logistic regression (pass `class_weight=\"balanced\"` to scikit-learn, or derive the gradient yourself). Measure the effect on a synthetic 90-10 class imbalance.\n3. **Hard.** Build a sarcasm detector by training a second classifier on the residuals of the sentiment model. Document your experimental setup. Warn the reader when your accuracy is below chance (chance-level on 2-class sarcasm is ~50%, and most first attempts land there)."
      ]
    }
